[36m(main_task pid=2091416)[0m Doc 4(Title: "Alexandra (given name)") Alexis (Πασκαλία, or Paskalia; Greek: Pascha, or Paskhia) is the name that is traditionally given to Jesus's mother Mary, in commemoration of His Resurrection (Pascha, or Paskhia, meaning, "Passover"). "Alexis" is a Greek name which means "defender of the people" or "help of the people". In Bulgaria, Poland, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, and Croatia, it is typically spelled "Aleska", also "Aleksa" in Slovenia; in other European countries the more usual spelling is "Alexandra". The name is derived from the Aramaic אַלְכָּסָא (al-kāsā) and Greek Ἀλεξάνδρα (Alexandra), both meaning "defender of men". It is common in the English-speaking world and one of the most famous bearers of the name was Queen Alexandra, wife of King Edward VII of the United Kingdom.
